<!-- S1: HERO: TypeSafe Jev -->
<section class="spread s1">
  <div class="spread-meta">
    <span class="spread-section">Models &middot; Software</span>
    <span class="article-no">N<sup>o</sup> I</span>
  </div>
  <p class="kicker">TypeSafe AI, September 15, 2026</p>
  <h1 class="s1-headline">A model that<br><em>gave up</em><br>the ability to write.</h1>
  <p class="s1-deck">TypeSafe AI released Jev on September 15 after two years in stealth. It returns typed decisions with confidence scores, charges $0.042 per million input tokens, and does not meter output. Its largest speed and cost claims come from tests the company built itself.</p>
  <div class="s1-byline">
    <span>By David Borish</span>
    <span>September 19, 2026 &middot; 6 min read</span>
  </div>
  <div class="s1-grid">
    <div class="s1-body">
      <p>Diogo Almeida helped build the instruction-following methods behind ChatGPT at OpenAI. He then spent four years asking why conversational skill never turned into widespread automation. His answer is a company called TypeSafe AI and a model called Jev, released September 15 after two years in stealth. The premise is that chat models are the wrong tool for decisions buried inside software. A chatbot can afford to be creative, occasionally wrong, and slow. Code that routes a support ticket or scores a transaction cannot.</p>
      <p>Jev gives up text generation entirely. Almeida describes it as a frontier-intelligence function call: unstructured state goes in, typed probabilistic decisions come out. The possible outputs are declared in advance, and the model fills them in with answers and confidence scores. Software no longer has to parse and validate a stream of prose. TypeSafe says Jev matches existing LLMs on what it calls System One tasks, the fast and intuitive kind in Kahneman&rsquo;s terms, while running two orders of magnitude faster and cheaper.</p>
      <p>The training method is called Reinforcement Learning for Calibrated Decisions. Standard models optimize for human preference or verifiable correctness. TypeSafe optimizes for calibration, so the probabilities the model reports track its actual accuracy. Its argument is that a model correct 95 percent of the time cannot safely automate a task if it cannot flag the other 5 percent.</p>
      <p>The claims a user can check directly are price and latency. Jev charges $0.042 per million input tokens and does not meter output. Frontier LLMs charge between $0.20 and $10 per million input tokens, with output priced roughly five times higher. TypeSafe reports response times of 70 to 500 milliseconds against 3 to 329 seconds for frontier models, or 40 to 200 times faster on comparable tasks. It says the pricing may or may not be subsidized and that only time will show whether it holds.</p>
      <p>The larger figures, 193.6 times faster and 444.6 times cheaper, come from a benchmark TypeSafe designed. Every model runs the same coded workflow and is scored against the average of GPT-6 Astra and Fable 5.1. TypeSafe discloses two sources of bias. Its own model capabilities team built the workflows, and using OpenAI and Anthropic models as the reference likely understates Jev. None of it has been independently tested.</p>
      <p>The type-safety claim is the cleanest. Outputs are constrained to a schema declared in advance, so TypeSafe says type errors are mathematically impossible, and it charts hallucination at zero on the same grounds. That follows from the schema, not from measured performance. A value that fits the schema is not necessarily a correct one, and Jev&rsquo;s accuracy on real workloads still has to be shown.</p>
      <p>The intended uses are smart if-statements: classify, route, score, extract, or branch where hand-written rules are too brittle. Jev cannot write prose, so it does not replace chatbots or coding agents. In one demo it played Doom from a text description of game state at about ten queries per second, for roughly $7 an hour. TypeSafe concedes a non-AI bot would play better. Early access is open, and results outside the company&rsquo;s own benchmarks remain to be established.</p>
    </div>
