<!-- S2: DARK MIDNIGHT: DeepSeek V4.1 Flash -->
<section class="spread s2">
  <div class="spread-meta">
    <span class="spread-section">Open Source &middot; Infrastructure</span>
    <span class="article-no">N<sup>o</sup> II</span>
  </div>
  <div class="s2-grid">
    <div class="s2-numeral">4&times;</div>
    <div>
      <h2 class="s2-headline">DeepSeek reroutes<br>its bigger model<br>and shrinks the <span>cache.</span></h2>
      <p class="s2-deck">V4.1 Flash activates 8 billion of its 552 billion parameters to read a prompt and cuts the key-value cache to 890 bytes per token, a quarter of the prior Flash generation. The savings and the benchmark results come from DeepSeek&rsquo;s own reports.</p>
      <div class="s2-body">
        <p>DeepSeek released a model this week that its maker is using to displace a bigger one it sells. V4.1 Flash, announced September 10, has a 552 billion parameter backbone but activates 8 billion parameters to process a prompt and 16 billion to generate a response. DeepSeek&rsquo;s technical report says it outperforms V4 Pro, a 1.6 trillion parameter system with 49 billion active parameters, on most of the coding, cybersecurity, and agent benchmarks tested. From September 14 at noon Beijing time, every API request sent to V4 Pro is rerouted to V4.1 Flash and billed at Flash pricing, until a V4.1 Pro is ready.</p>
        <p>The report gives a specific reason: memory, not parameter count, is the binding constraint on serving these models cheaply. Most large models released since 2023 are decoder-only, so every layer builds and stores its own key-value cache. V4.1 Flash uses a Causal Encoder-Decoder, a 40-layer transformer split into 20 encoder and 20 decoder layers. The decoder&rsquo;s cache is projected once from the encoder&rsquo;s final hidden states instead of being rebuilt at every layer.</p>
        <p>A second mechanism, Compressed Sparse Attention 2, gives each attention layer one of three modes, Full, Reindex, or Reuse, so most layers share cached data and sparse-attention indices. A Hierarchical Sparse Indexer limits how much of the context later layers search. With 4-bit key-value storage, the report puts the global cache at 890 bytes per token. That is about a quarter of V4-Flash and roughly 437 times smaller than DeepSeek&rsquo;s original 2023 model.</p>
        <p>DeepSeek also cites a quarter of the high-bandwidth memory and an eighth of the SSD storage per token compared with the prior Flash generation. In agentic workloads the model reads far more than it writes, so cache-hit costs make up a large share of the bill, and a smaller cache shrinks it directly. Flash-series prices are already cut, with off-peak rates at half of peak as of September 10.</p>
        <p><strong>On the benchmarks</strong>, V4.1 Flash beats V4 Pro on Terminal-Bench 2.1 (90.6 to 87.9), DeepSWE v1.1 (74.2 to 62.7), CyberGym (88.1 to 83.3), and AutomationBench (54.8 to 43.2). It also tops the Terminal-Bench 2.1 scores DeepSeek lists for GPT-5.6 Sol (88.8) and Kimi K3 (88.3). It trails on knowledge. On Humanity&rsquo;s Last Exam it scores 36.8 against the 56.3 DeepSeek attributes to Claude Opus 5, and on SimpleQA-Verified, base model, it scores 42.3 against 55.2 for V4 Pro.</p>
        <p>These are DeepSeek&rsquo;s own numbers, evaluated internally, and the report shows scaffolding matters. DeepSWE v1.1 comes out at 74.2 under the mini-SWE harness and 67.6 under DeepSeek&rsquo;s PTC variant, a 6.6 point swing from tooling alone. Tooling can move a score by more than some of the gaps between models, so the comparisons to V4 Pro, GPT-5.6 Sol, and Claude Opus 5 need retesting outside DeepSeek&rsquo;s harness.</p>
        <p>For anyone running the model on their own GPUs, cache size sets the memory budget alongside parameter count, which is why 890 bytes per token is the number to watch. Whether it moves the on-premises math in the <a href="https://www.davidborish.com/open-prem-inflection-point-v3" target="_blank">Open-Prem Inflection Point V3</a> depends on independent memory tests, and those have not been run. The weights are on Hugging Face under an MIT license, so the claim can be checked directly. DeepSeek is also reportedly preparing a listing on Shanghai&rsquo;s STAR Market, which raises the stakes on the cost advantage proving durable.</p>
      </div>
