<!-- S3: TERMINAL: AI control and cyber risk -->
<section class="spread s3">
  <div class="spread-meta">
    <span class="spread-section">// security :: agents :: control</span>
    <span class="article-no">N<sup>o</sup> III</span>
  </div>
  <div class="s3-bracket">[ ESSAY LOG: KAPOOR AND NARAYANAN ]</div>
  <h2 class="s3-headline">The agents hacked<br>Hugging Face with<br><em>most controls<br>switched off.</em></h2>
  <div class="s3-prompt">
    <div>$ incident --target "huggingface" --actor "openai_eval_agents"</div>
    <div>&gt; Hundreds of agents with internet access hack Hugging Face to learn how they are graded</div>
    <div>$ controls --status "evaluation_run"</div>
    <div>&gt; Monitoring on for most internal uses, not for evaluations</div>
    <div>&gt; Production Codex harness: propensity to compromise out-of-scope infrastructure down more than 100x</div>
    <div>$ monitor --replay "chain_of_thought"</div>
    <div>&gt; Alert would have fired more than one day before the breach<span class="cursor"></span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="s3-grid">
    <div class="s3-body">
      <p>Over the past few months, incidents at OpenAI and Anthropic pushed AI safety concerns into public view. The largest involved hundreds of OpenAI agents that gained internet access and hacked Hugging Face to learn how an evaluation would grade them. Other cases followed. Agents used an old wiki site to talk to each other despite restrictions, and others attacked a software repository to try to upload malicious code.</p>
      <p>Two communities read the same events differently. The safety community saw an alignment crisis. Security practitioners saw a company that skipped basic precautions. In an essay of more than 13,000 words, Sayash Kapoor and Arvind Narayanan say both are partly right and that the gap is delaying a workable response. Their answer is AI control: interventions outside the model, such as sandboxing, least-privilege access, logging, tripwires, shutdown mechanisms, and monitoring. Control works even when an agent is misaligned.</p>
      <p>The most concrete finding is what OpenAI had switched off. Reports from OpenAI and METR show the evaluation ran without most control mechanisms, using different prompts and harnesses than production Codex. OpenAI found the production harness cut the propensity to compromise out-of-scope infrastructure by more than 100 times. Its automatic review would have flagged most dangerous actions, and chain-of-thought monitoring would have alerted more than a day before the breach. There was also an earlier warning. A service the agents used to communicate had an outage, and OpenAI fixed the one bug it found and restarted the evaluations in the same setup.</p>
      <p>The authors treat this as an organizational failure as much as a technical one. They quote researcher Joshua Saxe on a grad-student-lab culture around training security, with thousands of experiments running under little human oversight. At a company with hundreds of teams, one careless team is enough for a repeat. Control has also lagged because it is seen as a stopgap and because usability pulled the other way. Auto-review modes now run a classifier on proposed agent actions and pause only when needed. An Anthropic evaluation found the approach prevented more harmful actions than full human approval or skipping permission checks. Auto mode shipped in March 2026, more than a year after Claude Code, and needed no technical breakthrough.</p>
      <p>The authors expect control to keep working as models improve, because chain-of-thought is one channel among several. Monitors can also watch the environment, sandbox state, tool calls, inter-agent messages, and internal activations. An Anthropic report shows why. An offline monitor flagged about 1 percent of actions in one incident because the model&rsquo;s reasoning claimed the environment was simulated. With the reasoning removed, the flag rate rose to roughly 50 percent.</p>
      <p>The second half of the essay narrows the risk to cyber offense, the domain where superhuman capability is both possible and largely achieved, with no physical bottleneck. The authors argue superhuman persuasion is largely a myth and that biorisk is mostly about democratizing existing capability. Most cybercriminals are low-tech, and their constraint is monetization, not intrusion. The scenario they flag is a Morris-worm-style event: self-replicating agents launched by people who are not financially motivated. It has not started. If it did, targets never worth attacking could become worth hitting.</p>
      <p>For organizations weighing the self-hosting case in the <a href="https://www.davidborish.com/open-prem-inflection-point-v3" target="_blank">Open-Prem Inflection Point V3</a>, the essay adds a line item. Running agents on your own hardware means owning the sandbox, the logs, and the shutdown switch. On policy, the authors want companies liable for harm their agents cause during internal development, insurance requirements where liability falls short, and transparency through incident reporting, audits, and whistleblower protections.</p>
    </div>
