<!-- S4: ACADEMIC: Dream-RSI -->
<section class="spread s4">
  <div class="spread-meta">
    <span class="spread-section">Research &middot; Recursive Self-Improvement</span>
    <span class="article-no">N<sup>o</sup> IV</span>
  </div>
  <h2 class="s4-headline">Replaying History<br><em>Makes Search Strategies</em><br>Cheap to Test</h2>
  <div class="s4-rule">&sect; &sect; &sect;</div>
  <p class="s4-deck">Dream-RSI, a preprint from Google, Google DeepMind, and two universities, scores new search strategies against the record of earlier discovery runs instead of running them for real. It cut agent calls on eight tasks, but its comparisons rest on figures from other papers.</p>
  <div class="s4-body">
    <p>Getting a coding agent to find a faster algorithm, a better mathematical construction, or a more efficient GPU kernel usually means running it thousands of times. It proposes a candidate, tests it, learns from the result, and proposes again. The loop works but improves slowly, because the part worth improving, the search strategy, is expensive to evaluate. Judging a new way to allocate search effort means watching it play out across a whole discovery run, which can take hundreds or thousands of cycles.</p>
    <p>A paper posted to arXiv on September 14, 2026 proposes a way around this. Dream-RSI comes from researchers at Google and Google DeepMind, with collaborators at the University of Maryland and the University of Virginia. It treats the record of everything a search process has already tried as a reusable simulator. A new strategy is replayed against history that has already been collected, at no execution cost.</p>
    <p><strong>The idea</strong> comes from model-based reinforcement learning, the Dreamer line in particular, where an agent improves by imagining outcomes in a learned model. The authors argue a completed discovery run already provides an equivalent. Every proposal, score, and dead end is recorded in a tree. An alternative strategy can be run against that tree and scored on how it would have performed, without generating a new candidate or calling the coding agent again.</p>
    <p>The system runs in three steps. In the online phase the current strategy directs a real run, and the resulting tree joins a growing pool of replay simulators. In the offline phase the system generates a large batch of candidate revisions and scores each by replaying it against that pool. The best version directs the next real run. The coding agent, the evaluator, and the models stay fixed. Only the code that allocates search effort changes.</p>
    <p><strong>In algorithm engineering</strong>, the task was a solver for the Lasso regularization path. With Gemini 3.1 Pro as the coding agent, Dream-RSI cut agent calls from 550 to 317 across five rounds and lowered average runtime on six held-out datasets from 3,587.1 to 2,931.0 milliseconds. With Gemini 3.7 Flash, calls fell from 3,200 to 1,879. Against SimpleTES, which used 51,200 generations, Dream-RSI reached faster downstream performance with roughly 162 times fewer discovery-agent calls.</p>
    <p>In mathematical optimization, covering the sum-difference problem, autocorrelation inequalities, and circle packing, it matched or edged out AlphaEvolve and AlphaEvolveV2 with under 1,000 generations. On autocorrelation, SimpleTES posted the best published score but needed 51,200 generations. On four KernelBench GPU tasks, Dream-RSI matched the fixed-strategy baseline with 2.43 times fewer generations on a VGG16 kernel and 1.79 times fewer on LayerNorm. On ConvDiv and ConvMax it reached 2.09 and 1.44 times higher performance at the same budget.</p>
    <p><strong>One result cuts against the obvious fix.</strong> Injecting explicit directional hints into the discovery agent&rsquo;s prompt made both Dream-RSI and the baseline perform worse. The paper reads this as strong semantic steering narrowing exploration in a process that already runs many parallel threads. In a separate analysis of one kernel task, the learned strategy cut candidates evaluated per round from 110 to 50 while performance climbed, then raised the number again once progress leveled off, and further gains followed.</p>
    <p>The limits are stated plainly. Dream-RSI is a preprint and has not been peer reviewed. Its comparisons to SimpleTES, AlphaEvolve, and other systems use figures from those systems&rsquo; own papers, not results reproduced on matching hardware. The repository holds the paper and a project page, with the code, reproduction scripts, and discovered programs listed as in progress. Whether the mechanism holds on messier discovery problems with less clean scoring functions is the open question.</p>
  </div>
