<!-- S5: RED LEDGER: Latham & Watkins -->
<section class="spread s5">
  <div class="spread-meta">
    <span class="spread-section">Law &middot; Economics &middot; Infrastructure</span>
    <span class="article-no">N<sup>o</sup> V</span>
  </div>
  <div class="s5-grid">
    <div>
      <h2 class="s5-headline">The second-largest<br>U.S. law firm<br>buys its own <span>GPUs.</span></h2>
      <p class="s5-deck">Latham &amp; Watkins is running and customizing open-weight models on its own Nvidia servers. Its chief information officer cites two reasons: client confidentiality and exposure to token pricing once subsidies narrow.</p>
      <div class="s5-body">
        <p>Latham &amp; Watkins, the second-largest U.S. law firm by revenue at $8.3 billion last year, has bought servers loaded with multiple Nvidia GPUs and is using them to run and customize open-weight models inside its own systems. Financial Times reporting, picked up by Law360 and Legal IT Insider, describes it as the first major law firm known to build this infrastructure itself. Most large firms layer their own workflows on someone else&rsquo;s compute and model weights, or subscribe to platforms such as Harvey and Legora.</p>
        <p>Chief information officer Rene Mendoza gave two reasons. Some client matters are too sensitive for any outside party, and in those cases, he said, &ldquo;we don&rsquo;t want to put it to any cloud vendor.&rdquo; The second is pricing. AI providers have subsidized token costs to win enterprise customers, and Mendoza wants Latham insulated when usage-based billing takes over. The backdrop is pressure from corporate clients, banks in particular, to hold or cut fees even as AI shrinks the work.</p>
        <p>The same calculation shows up elsewhere. Bristol Myers Squibb runs Nvidia DGX SuperPOD infrastructure for drug discovery through Equinix Private AI, keeps cloud for overflow, and reports 55 percent lower costs than its previous setup. Lockheed Martin has consolidated more than 30 models onto an on-premises AI Factory serving roughly 7,000 engineers, and Nvidia says it runs at full utilization. BNY&rsquo;s Eliza platform supports more than 17,000 users. Texas A&amp;M&rsquo;s research cluster runs nearly 760 GPUs at 95 to 98 percent capacity.</p>
        <p>None of them left the cloud. They separated steady, high-volume baseline work from unpredictable spikes and short experiments, and rent only the latter. AWS lists B200 capacity at roughly $12.36 per accelerator hour and H100s near $5.19 in major U.S. regions. Run continuously for a year, that is about $108,000 per B200 and $45,000 per H100.</p>
        <p>Vendor estimates point the same way and should be read as vendor estimates. Lenovo says some sustained inference deployments break even against hyperscale cloud in as little as six months and cost up to seventeen times less per million tokens. AMD models a roughly even local and cloud split cutting three-year costs by 40 to 60 percent against cloud only, with fully local break-even in under two years. Dell reports more than $130 billion in AI server orders over the past twelve months.</p>
        <p>This is the convergence the <a href="https://www.davidborish.com/open-prem-inflection-point-v3" target="_blank">Open-Prem Inflection Point V3</a> describes: open-weight performance, falling hardware costs, and data sovereignty lining up so that self-hosting becomes the rational default for organizations with steady, high-volume usage and sensitive data. The April 2026 update counted nine or more open-weight model families at or near frontier performance and put self-hosted inference at five to twenty cents per million tokens, against three to fifteen dollars for proprietary cloud APIs. It estimated that organizations processing more than two million tokens a day could recover hardware costs in six to twelve months. Latham&rsquo;s two reasons match the framework&rsquo;s strongest pulls: sensitive data and exposure to consumption pricing.</p>
        <p>The costs move inside the firm. Latham now runs cybersecurity, cooling, power, networking, and hardware refresh itself, which Legal IT Insider flagged as requiring substantial capital and specialist expertise. Utilization decides whether that pays, and a firm with idle GPUs is better off renting. Firms without Latham&rsquo;s scale may find a hybrid path more realistic: modest owned hardware for steady work and cloud for spikes, the split Bristol Myers Squibb runs in production.</p>
      </div>
